# shutdown — real-world examples

## Reboot immediately with a wall message

```sh
sudo shutdown -r now "Rebooting mediaserver for kernel update"
```

## Reboot in one minute (gives users time to save)

```sh
sudo shutdown -r +1 "Going down in 1 minute"
```

## Power off now

```sh
sudo shutdown -P now
```

## Schedule a power-off for a specific time

```sh
sudo shutdown -P 23:30 "Nightly power-down"
```

## Cancel a pending shutdown

```sh
sudo shutdown -c
```

## Flag reference

| Flag  | What it does                       |
| ----- | ---------------------------------- |
| -r    | reboot                             |
| -P    | power off                          |
| -H    | halt                               |
| -c    | cancel a scheduled shutdown        |
| now   | act immediately                    |
| +N    | act in N minutes                   |

> On systemd (Tumbleweed), `shutdown` is provided by systemd and
> `systemctl reboot` / `systemctl poweroff` do the same job.
